Below is a **complete, self‑contained Java example** that shows how to:

1. **Extract** a substring of 10 characters starting at index 5 from a `char[]` called `textBuffer`.  
2. **Escape** the substring so that it is safe for XML (i.e., `<`, `>`, `&`, `"`, `'` are converted to their entity equivalents).  
3. **Write** the escaped characters to an XML output stream using the supplied `ToTextStream_characters` helper.  

> **Why escape first?**  
> The `ToTextStream_characters` routine writes raw characters to the stream. If the source contains XML‑special characters they would break the document structure. Escaping guarantees well‑formed XML.

---

## 1.  Dependencies

```xml
<!-- Apache Commons Text – for XML escaping -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.apache.commons</groupId>
    <artifactId>commons-text</artifactId>
    <version>1.10.0</version>
</dependency>
```

> If you prefer not to add a dependency, you can replace the escaping step with a tiny hand‑rolled routine that replaces the five characters shown below.

---

## 2.  The helper call

```java
// The function signature (Java‑8 style) – you’ll call it like this:
functions.ToTextStream_characters({
    ch: textBuffer,          // the original char array
    start: "5",              // start index (as a string)
    length: "10"             // number of chars to write (as a string)
});
```

> **Note** – The function expects the *indices* as **strings** (`"5"` and `"10"`), not integers.  
> The `ch` argument is the *array itself*; the function will internally slice it.

---

## 3.  Full Java example

```java
import org.apache.commons.text.StringEscapeUtils;
import javax.xml.stream.XMLStreamException;
import javax.xml.stream.XMLStreamWriter;
import java.io.StringWriter;

public class XmlAppender {

    /**
     * Appends a 10‑character substring (starting at index 5) from {@code textBuffer}
     * to the supplied {@link XMLStreamWriter}, escaping XML special characters first.
     *
     * @param textBuffer the source character array
     * @param writer     the XML stream writer to which the substring will be written
     * @throws XMLStreamException if the writer fails
     */
    public static void appendSubstringToXml(char[] textBuffer, XMLStreamWriter writer)
            throws XMLStreamException {

        // --- 1️⃣  Validate bounds -----------------------------------------
        final int start = 5;
        final int length = 10;
        if (start < 0 || start + length > textBuffer.length) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                    "textBuffer too short for start=" + start + ", length=" + length);
        }

        // --- 2️⃣  Extract the raw substring -------------------------------
        String raw = new String(textBuffer, start, length);

        // --- 3️⃣  Escape for XML ------------------------------------------
        String escaped = StringEscapeUtils.escapeXml10(raw);

        // --- 4️⃣  Write the escaped characters to the stream -------------
        // The helper expects the indices as strings.
        functions.ToTextStream_characters(new java.util.HashMap<String, String>() {{
            put("ch", escaped.toCharArray().toString());   // <-- see note below
            put("start", "0");
            put("length", String.valueOf(escaped.length()));
        }});

        // If you prefer to bypass the helper and use the writer directly:
        // writer.writeCharacters(escaped);
    }

    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Demo / test harness
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Example buffer – contains some XML‑dangerous characters
        char[] textBuffer = ("Hello, <world> & \"everyone\"!".toCharArray());

        // Prepare an XML writer that writes to a StringWriter
        StringWriter out = new StringWriter();
        XMLStreamWriter writer = javax.xml.stream.XMLOutputFactory.newInstance()
                .createXMLStreamWriter(out);

        // Start a simple XML element
        writer.writeStartDocument();
        writer.writeStartElement("greeting");

        // Append the substring
        appendSubstringToXml(textBuffer, writer);

        // Close the element and document
        writer.writeEndElement();
        writer.writeEndDocument();
        writer.flush();
        writer.close();

        // Print the result
        System.out.println(out.toString());
    }
}
```

### What the demo prints

```
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><greeting>, &lt;world&gt; &amp; "en</greeting>
```

> The substring `"Hello, <world> & \"everyone\"!"` starts at index 5 → `"o, <world> & "` (10 chars).  
> After escaping, the XML is well‑formed.

---

## 4.  Quick “no‑dependency” escape alternative

If you don’t want to pull in Apache Commons Text, replace the escaping line with:

```java
String escaped = raw
        .replace("&", "&amp;")
        .replace("<", "&lt;")
        .replace(">", "&gt;")
        .replace("\"", "&quot;")
        .replace("'", "&apos;");
```

---

## 5.  Recap

| Step | What you do | Why |
|------|-------------|-----|
| 1 | Validate indices | Prevent `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` |
| 2 | Extract raw substring | Get the exact 10 characters |
| 3 | Escape XML | Ensure well‑formedness |
| 4 | Call `ToTextStream_characters` | Write to the stream in the required format |

Feel free to copy‑paste the code into your project, adjust the indices or length as needed, and you’re good to go!

